.Dd October 15, 2026
.Dt TRUNCATE 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm truncate ,
.Nm truncate64 ,
.Nm ftruncate ,
.Nm ftruncate64
.Nd change the length of a file
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft int
.Fn truncate "const char *path" "off_t length"
.Ft int
.Fn ftruncate "int fd" "off_t length"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn truncate
sets the length of the file at
.Fa path ,
and
.Fn ftruncate
the length of the file
.Fa fd
is open on, to
.Fa length
bytes.
KRFFS, FAT32, exFAT, and ext2 support both shrinking and extending files.
An extended region reads as zeros.
A file attached to a loop device cannot be truncated, even to its
current length.
.Pp
After a successful call by a process whose effective uid is not 0,
the set-user-ID bit is cleared.
The set-group-ID bit is cleared if group execute permission is set.
.Pp
KRFFS and FAT32 accept lengths through 4294967295 bytes, subject to
available space.
The exFAT length limit is 9223372036854775807 bytes.
The ext2 limit depends on the volume's block size and is bounded by
its block pointers and 32-bit sector count.
The file system refuses a length beyond its limit.
.Ss Truncating by path
.Fn truncate
resolves
.Fa path
through symbolic links, a relative path from the working directory,
and makes its checks in Linux's order.
A path that ends in a slash, or whose last component is
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&.. ,
must reach a directory, which returns
.Er EISDIR
as any directory does, and anything else returns
.Er ENOTDIR ,
as on Linux.
A negative length returns
.Er EINVAL
before the path is looked at.
A directory returns
.Er EISDIR ,
any other file that is not a regular file
.Er EINVAL ,
a file on a read-only mount
.Er EROFS ,
and a file the caller may not write
.Er EACCES .
A file under
.Pa /proc
returns
.Er EACCES
to every caller, as an open of one for writing does; Linux lets root
truncate one and changes nothing.
A file backing a loop device returns
.Er EBUSY .
Linux has no such rule, and returns
.Er ETXTBSY
for a program being run, which this kernel does not.
.Pp
The file is truncated through an open-file description that no
descriptor refers to, so the call does what an open for writing,
.Fn ftruncate
and
.Xr close 2
do, without taking a descriptor: an attachment of the file to a loop
device made meanwhile returns
.Er EBUSY ,
as it does during an
.Fn ftruncate .
.Ss Truncating a descriptor
.Fn ftruncate
makes its checks in Linux's order.
A negative length returns
.Er EINVAL
before the descriptor is looked at.
A descriptor that is not open, or was opened with
.Dv O_PATH ,
returns
.Er EBADF .
The descriptor must refer to a file on a mounted file system, open for
writing; a directory, a pipe, a socket, a device, or a file open only
for reading returns
.Er EINVAL ,
as on Linux.
.Ss System call interface
Musl's
.Vt off_t
is 64 bits on i386.
Its
.Fn truncate
uses system call 193,
.Nm truncate64 ,
and its
.Fn ftruncate
system call 194,
.Nm ftruncate64 ;
GNU libc uses the same two calls.
EBX is the path or the descriptor; ECX and EDX are the low and high
32-bit halves of the length.
With
.Dv _LARGEFILE64_SOURCE
defined, musl defines
.Nm truncate64
and
.Nm ftruncate64
as macros for
.Fn truncate
and
.Fn ftruncate ;
there are no separate prototypes.
.Pp
The older system calls 92,
.Nm truncate ,
and 93,
.Nm ftruncate ,
take the path or the descriptor in EBX and a 32-bit length in ECX.
Both read the length as signed, as Linux does, so a negative length
returns
.Er EINVAL ,
as a 64-bit length with its high bit set does through calls 193 and
194.
.Pp
On x86-64
.Nm truncate
(76) and
.Nm ftruncate
(77) take the 64-bit length in RSI, and there are no 64-suffixed calls.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn truncate
and
.Fn ftruncate
return 0 on success, or -1 on failure with
.Va errno
set.
The kernel returns a negative error number on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/vfsprobe.c
opens a three-byte file for reading and writing, truncates it to one
byte, and checks that a seek to the end returns 1.
.Pa tests/musl/truncmusl.c
shortens and lengthens a file by path through both system calls,
checks the zeros an extension reads as and a descriptor open on the
file, drops the set-id bits as an unprivileged caller, and asks for
each refusal of both functions in order.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width ENAMETOOLONG
.It Er EACCES
.Fn truncate
was denied search permission on a directory in the path or write
permission on the file, or the file is under
.Pa /proc .
.It Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not an open descriptor, was opened with
.Dv O_PATH ,
or has no mounted open-file description.
.It Er EBUSY
The file backs an attached loop device, or path resolution enters a
mount being mounted or unmounted.
.It Er EFAULT
The path cannot be read from user memory.
.It Er EFBIG
The requested length exceeds the file system's limit.
.It Er EINVAL
The length is negative.
.Fn truncate
was given a file that is not a regular file, or
.Fn ftruncate
a descriptor that does not refer to a file of a mounted file system or
is not open for writing.
ext2 was given a nonregular file.
.It Er EIO
The device was removed, an input/output operation failed, or KRFFS
could not resolve or resize the file.
.It Er EISDIR
.Fn truncate
was given a directory.
.It Er ELOOP
Path resolution would follow more than 40 symbolic links.
.It Er ENAMETOOLONG
The path or its joined absolute form exceeds 4095 bytes, a component
exceeds 255 bytes, or symbolic-link expansion exceeds 4095 bytes.
.It Er ENOENT
The path is empty, a component is missing, or a symbolic link has an
empty target.
exFAT could not find the containing directory while updating the file's
directory entries.
.It Er ENOMEM
No memory is left for a path page or an open-file description, or
exFAT could not allocate a node for the containing directory.
.It Er ENOSPC
A KRFFS, FAT32, or exFAT file system has insufficient space to extend
the file.
.It Er ENOTDIR
A path component used as a directory is not a directory, or a path that
ends in a slash or in
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&..
reaches something else.
.It Er ENXIO
A FAT32 or exFAT cluster chain ends before a required cluster.
.It Er EPERM
The file is immutable or append-only.
.It Er EROFS
The file system is read-only or has no truncate operation.
.It Er ESRCH
There is no current process.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr open 2 ,
.Xr stat 2 ,
.Xr write 2
